1) Likely causes (short)

Pressure model vs flow dimensionality: your code computes an Ergun pressure based on flow in z and uses that pressure to accelerate particles in x/y (as your prof described). That gives a pressure field, but it doesn't produce lateral fluid velocity unless you solve the full 3D fluid flow. The experiment has 3-D lateral flow structures → mismatch.

How multi-sphere bodies are counted in porosity (VF): if each sub-sphere is counted separately when computing local VF, you may overcount solids (or undercount the actual effective porosity of a multi-sphere body), producing wrong Ergun pressure.

Singular behaviour at low porosity: Ergun ∝ (1/ε^3) so if ε→0 locally (e.g. along particle edges) you get huge/invalid pressure unless clipped/regularized.

Hydrodynamic interactions missing: lubrication/added mass/pore flow (Darcy) effects can be important for hollow/polygons.

Filtering length & coupling frequency: too-small filter width or too-infrequent coupling can give noisy/incorrect pressure gradients.

2D vs 3D physics: a 2D approximation may not allow lateral fluid motion that is present experimentally.
